{:edb/db-before-t 2879 :edb/db-after-t 2880 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536192 50 #inst "2026-10-09T19:08:38.455Z" 13194139536192 true] [17592186063913 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" 13194139536192 false] [17592186063913 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/8fb287885c948b7e563b5fa878dd204dacbedea311490c4573aa883bff992662.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y=f^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2, 0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$\nThe point $B$ has coordinates $(0,2)$ and lies on $y=f^{-1}(x).$ If $f'(2)=-\\dfrac{3}{2},$ what is $(f^{-1})'(0)?$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388176 13194139536192 true] [17592186064163 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388183 13194139536192 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388190 13194139536192 true] [17592186064164 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388198 13194139536192 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388148 13194139536192 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388155 13194139536192 true] [17592186064165 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388202 13194139536192 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388162 13194139536192 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388169 13194139536192 true] [17592186064166 1020 #edb/ref 17592186388206 13194139536192 true] [17592186064435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186064435 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/24d83be9fed627018111aff41b976881c98c6c58b2ceeb0ff11c033f9926afd3.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(3,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,3)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186064435 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/291/Source/Images/q-15667.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y = f(x)$. The point $A$ has coordinates $(3, 2)$ and lies on $y = f(x)$. Given that $(2, 3)$ lies on $y = f^{-1}(x)$, what is $(f^{-1})^{′}(2)$?" 13194139536192 false] [17592186064435 1145 "Since $(2,3)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(3)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186064534 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536192 true] [17592186064534 1100 "Consider the function $f(x) = x^{3} + 3$ and the point $P(2, 11)$ that lies on $y = f(x)$. Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P$, calculate $(f^{-1})^{′}(11)$." 13194139536192 false] [17592186064534 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3$ and the point $P(2,11)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11).$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186064534 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,11).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{3}+3 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =12\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,11)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(11,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(11) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(11))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186295808 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536192 false] [17592186295808 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186295822 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536192 false] [17592186295822 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14083756/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186295823 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388197 13194139536192 true] [17592186295833 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388201 13194139536192 true] [17592186388148 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388149 13194139536192 true] [17592186388148 1089 "q-110307" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388148 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186388148 1100 "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)$  of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n| $-3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388148 1113 #uuid "728516cc-a318-5bc6-87a9-bed32f2e0ec8" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388148 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4)={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(4)=-3,$ $g(4)=3,$ ${g}^{′}(4)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(4) & ={g}^{′}(4){f}^{-1}(4)+\\frac{g(4)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{3}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-3+\\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{12}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388150 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388151 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388152 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388153 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388154 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1041 "selection" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388153 13194139536192 true] [17592186388149 1110 #uuid "64bafd13-4937-5c24-b2ed-715d4d5a8781" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388150 1034 "-\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388150 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388150 1111 #uuid "c92cfc0c-0b9d-5ea7-b010-72abd6d1d49a" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388151 1034 "\\frac{11}{5}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388151 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388151 1111 #uuid "157fc464-b501-5e51-9aa0-735d660c5d85" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388152 1034 "\\frac{6}{5}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388152 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388152 1111 #uuid "f48bbaab-5ab6-5c21-baf4-cca447254bb5" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388153 1034 "-\\frac{12}{5}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388153 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388153 1111 #uuid "b908e1e5-7546-541e-8b71-08859b50e757" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388154 1034 "-\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388154 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388154 1111 #uuid "e98de654-05d8-50a7-b097-e31f2fd0c5a7" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388155 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388156 13194139536192 true] [17592186388155 1089 "q-110308" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388155 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186388155 1100 "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(3)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $3$ | $5$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-2$ | $3$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388155 1113 #uuid "9f7f5d03-a853-5e3f-88b3-f143c3384e25" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388155 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(3)={g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(3)=-2,$ $g(3)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(3)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-2)=-4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(3) & ={g}^{′}(3){f}^{-1}(3)+\\frac{g(3)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(3))} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)+\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-2)} \\\\  & =-2+\\frac{(-3)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388157 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388158 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388159 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388160 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388161 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1041 "selection" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388159 13194139536192 true] [17592186388156 1110 #uuid "efdfbf35-7dd9-5182-8244-71da7be1b9b8" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388157 1034 "-\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388157 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388157 1111 #uuid "32283e5a-58c1-5838-ae16-131a056cae36" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388158 1034 "\\frac{4}{5}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388158 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388158 1111 #uuid "0443ee2b-27c2-5660-b22f-c7e876d28e6e" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388159 1034 "-\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388159 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388159 1111 #uuid "27b756d2-b835-573f-844a-24dd03d31203" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388160 1034 "\\frac{5}{4}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388160 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388160 1111 #uuid "b7575923-a50b-5387-8c92-29289d6ae0e4" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388161 1034 "-\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388161 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388161 1111 #uuid "270648f4-f099-56ad-a495-b9645d40f66a" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388162 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388163 13194139536192 true] [17592186388162 1089 "q-110345" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388162 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186388162 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={2}^{x}-7.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(9).$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388162 1113 #uuid "ab71d05c-b918-52c2-bf47-2e847a369a63" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388162 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(9),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(9)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(9))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(9).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(9)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(9) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =9 \\\\ {2}^{x}-7 & =9 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =16 \\\\ {2}^{x} & ={2}^{4} \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(9)=4.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(4),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={2}^{x}-7 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\cdot {2}^{4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =16\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(9) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(9))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388164 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388165 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388166 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388167 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388168 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1041 "selection" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388166 13194139536192 true] [17592186388163 1110 #uuid "32082d83-d392-5ea3-8624-0af973a39806" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388164 1034 "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388164 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388164 1111 #uuid "b9773492-dd72-5047-9ee5-cb02d71d9e6d" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388165 1034 "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388165 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388165 1111 #uuid "eacd902e-01d0-5056-8826-5245394852c2" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388166 1034 "\\frac{1}{16\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388166 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388166 1111 #uuid "d0d77215-4d73-53d9-bb8d-66dbeec46e0e" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388167 1034 "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388167 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388167 1111 #uuid "345f13cb-9c41-55bd-b227-0e7bd5ad4c57" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388168 1034 "\\frac{1}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388168 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388168 1111 #uuid "b06a95dc-1400-50f5-8af4-7eda09d1a504" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388169 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388170 13194139536192 true] [17592186388169 1089 "q-110497" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388169 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186388169 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)={3}^{x}-1.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8).$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388169 1113 #uuid "cd6d2ebf-c817-5eeb-8275-41ef01d7d0f4" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388169 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(8),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(8).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(8)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(8) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x}-1 & =8 \\\\ {3}^{x} & =9 \\\\ {3}^{x} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(8)=2.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={3}^{x}-1 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =9\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(8) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(8))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388171 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388172 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388173 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388174 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388175 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1041 "selection" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388175 13194139536192 true] [17592186388170 1110 #uuid "37c6be68-8840-526d-bb62-7a0a60f78f74" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388171 1034 "\\frac{1}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388171 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388171 1111 #uuid "480b4459-f881-5842-b5e9-cea3977473e8" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388172 1034 "\\frac{1}{9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388172 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388172 1