8187 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388187 1111 #uuid "2246cda6-4be2-516f-bcb4-ca244d23ba68" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388188 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388188 1111 #uuid "ff8b1edd-a11b-5cd3-a263-27bbffb1d39a" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388189 1034 "0" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388189 1111 #uuid "82c76660-31ae-5aff-b80d-41e76740bcdf" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388190 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388191 13194139536192 true] [17592186388190 1089 "q-2526" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388190 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186388190 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388190 1113 #uuid "469ade2f-f4b3-58d6-a683-fc6ee5ddd73f" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388190 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+3x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =2\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2})$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388192 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388193 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388194 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388195 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388196 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1041 "selection" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388193 13194139536192 true] [17592186388191 1110 #uuid "1a1d767c-4ca6-570e-8141-da96224c934b" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388192 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388192 1111 #uuid "9ac85305-fe64-5710-a7c0-b59a5a1fac45" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388193 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388193 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388193 1111 #uuid "c01b9e4d-9150-583f-9000-e05e952acf6e" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388194 1034 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388194 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388194 1111 #uuid "e44184de-708c-556e-b483-1c70619b588d" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388195 1034 "1" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388195 1111 #uuid "7224e289-6af6-598b-acd8-8752ff81d921" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388196 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388196 1111 #uuid "057a795f-6f79-5b8c-8096-4ddee3f29aee" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388197 1034 "2" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388197 1111 #uuid "9279453a-70a9-5b54-8d67-c3311cf7e8b3" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388198 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388199 13194139536192 true] [17592186388198 1089 "q-342581" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388198 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186388198 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x}$ and the point $P(4,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,4)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." 13194139536192 true] [17592186388198 1113 #uuid "6cd4a653-e15a-56e6-a54e-5c8af74b1421" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388198 1145 "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(4,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-1/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{4}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(4,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388199 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388200 13194139536192 true] [17592186388199 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536192 true] [17592186388199 1041 "field-1" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388199 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388200 13194139536192 true] [17592186388199 1110 #uuid "c90ef847-b4bf-5479-a810-e9c0d1177d9d" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388200 1034 "8" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388200 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388200 1111 #uuid "7f2a9871-30c7-5f85-8f7c-27bc28d08d91" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388201 1034 "2" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388201 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388201 1111 #uuid "0998d20c-9922-58be-aec2-a73bf65cba6b" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388202 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388203 13194139536192 true] [17592186388202 1089 "q-342597" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388202 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186388202 1100 "Find ${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)$ of the product of functions, given the following table of values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $-3$ | $0$ | $3$ | $-2$ | $4$ |\n\n${(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})}^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388202 1113 #uuid "41d98065-acca-5eb2-82b3-4c1ae5f6a004" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388202 1145 "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function $g(x){f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(x)={g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x)+\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0)={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(0)=-3,$ $g(0)=1,$ ${g}^{′}(0)=\\frac{1}{3},$ and ${f}^{′}(-3)=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(g\\cdot {f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & ={g}^{′}(0){f}^{-1}(0)+\\frac{g(0)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-3)+\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-3)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388204 13194139536192 true] [17592186388203 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388205 13194139536192 true] [17592186388203 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536192 true] [17592186388203 1041 "field-1" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388203 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388204 13194139536192 true] [17592186388203 1110 #uuid "c9d1ac83-e100-52eb-aaef-8007d5f0ddcf" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388204 1034 "-\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388204 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388204 1111 #uuid "4c011dca-d08a-55e4-a0ce-0ee30927aa38" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388205 1034 "1" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388205 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536192 true] [17592186388205 1111 #uuid "3c58aabf-fc98-57de-8141-7ea37cba24d9" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388206 1017 #edb/ref 17592186388207 13194139536192 true] [17592186388206 1089 "q-76115" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388206 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139536192 true] [17592186388206 1100 "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1).$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388206 1113 #uuid "7f01d7ed-1d8a-5bb3-a9a6-4686958c8b4d" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388206 1145 "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ For $x>\\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} & =1 \\\\ 1 & =\\sqrt{2x-1} \\\\ 1 & =2x-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{′}(1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2x-1}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(2x-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-\\frac{1}{(2\\cdot 1-1)^{3/2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(1) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388208 13194139536192 true] [17592186388207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388209 13194139536192 true] [17592186388207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388210 13194139536192 true] [17592186388207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388211 13194139536192 true] [17592186388207 1000 #edb/ref 17592186388212 13194139536192 true] [17592186388207 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536192 true] [17592186388207 1041 "selection" 13194139536192 true] [17592186388207 1104 #edb/ref 17592186388211 13194139536192 t